    overview: "The final section makes a demanding but realistic distinction. An involuntary natural inclination may arise, especially toward someone who has caused harm. What is required is to oppose that inclination inwardly, refuse to approve it, and keep voluntary speech and action from obeying it. Ghazali distinguishes three states by how the person relates to the unwanted wish.",
    reflection: "When the unwanted wish appears, add a second response: 'I do not consent to this, and I will not feed it.' Then choose the next action that proves the second response is governing.",
